.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH qsh-tunnel-open 1  "open 0.5.8" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-tunnel\-open \- Open a tunnel to a pinned host and hold it open
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh tunnel open\fR [\fB\-J \fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-L\fR|\fB\-\-local\fR] [\fB\-R\fR|\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-D\fR|\fB\-\-dynamic\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR] [\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-supervise\fR] [\fB\-\-accept\-hold\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR] [\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR] [\fB\-\-jump\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIHOST\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Open a tunnel to a pinned host and hold it open.
.PP
This is a *value* operation that then blocks: the `Tunnel` envelope is emitted once, and the tunnel lives for as long as this foreground process does (`docs/CLI.md` §6.14). Ctrl\-C ends it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-J\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Reach the host through these pinned peers for this command, `ssh \-J` spelled: `a` or `a,b`, or `none` to go the ordinary way (ADR\-0056). Same as `\-\-jump`
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-local\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Local forward `[bind:]listen_port:host:host_port` — same grammar as the interactive `\-L` (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Remote forward `[bind:]rport:host:hport` — same grammar as the interactive `\-R` (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-dynamic\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
SOCKS5 dynamic forwarding `[bind:]port`: open a SOCKS5 proxy on this machine and, for every CONNECT a SOCKS client sends it, forward to whatever destination that CONNECT names, as seen from the peer (ADR\-0019). Exactly one of `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote`/`\-\-dynamic` is required, and `\-\-dynamic` conflicts with the other two — a single `tunnel open` call opens exactly one listener.

Still `Vec<String>`/`ArgAction::Append` so the value can be given more than once on the command line, but only ever one listener is meant to result: giving `\-\-dynamic` twice is refused with `INVALID_ARGUMENT` ("one listener per `tunnel open`"), not silently collapsed to the first or last value.

There is no ACL check at open time: each CONNECT is authorized on the peer as `forward.local`, the same action `\-\-local` uses. `DYNAMIC_FORWARD_ACL_NOTE` — "`\-D` runs SOCKS5 on this machine and authorizes every CONNECT on the peer as `forward.local`; `forward.socks` is never consulted." Works over both forward and reverse routes (ADR\-0020 decisions 1–3) — a reverse route relays each CONNECT through this machine\*(Aqs resident `qsh listen` daemon, with the same host\-local address filter a forward route enforces. The one refusal left is `UNSUPPORTED` before anything binds when the connected route never negotiated `dial\-filter.v1`; on a reverse route the message names both possible causes (the target\*(Aqs qsh may predate the capability, or this machine\*(Aqs own `qsh listen` daemon may have started before this machine\*(Aqs own upgrade) (`docs/CLI.md` §6.9).
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR
Never run ssh on this command\*(Aqs behalf: no automatic bootstrap of an unknown name, and no restarting of a pinned host\*(Aqs serve over ssh when the connection fails (`docs/CLI.md` §7). `qsh pair ssh` is not affected; it is ssh by definition
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\fR \fI<MS>\fR [default: 0]
Milliseconds to keep retrying the initial route resolution while it answers the reverse\-registration\-stale branch. `0` (the default) is one attempt, unchanged from before this flag existed. Applies to `\-\-local`/`\-\-remote` only — combining it with `\-\-dynamic` is a clap usage error. Bound `0..=600000`; above it is `INVALID_ARGUMENT`. Full semantics: `docs/CLI.md` §6.9
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR
When the ssh bootstrap finds no `qsh` on the remote host (or one too old to answer it), install this machine\*(Aqs release into `~/.local/bin` there without asking (`docs/CLI.md` §7). Works with `\-\-json`
.TP
\fB\-\-supervise\fR \fI<MS>\fR [default: 0]
Keep the tunnel across connection loss: re\-establish it, for up to this many milliseconds of total disconnected time, instead of ending with the connection (ADR\-0023). `0` (the default) is today\*(Aqs behavior. Bound `0..=86400000`; above it is `INVALID_ARGUMENT`. Standalone `tunnel open` only; the interactive form has no such flag. Full semantics: `docs/CLI.md` §6.9
.TP
\fB\-\-accept\-hold\fR \fI<MS>\fR [default: 0]
While a supervised tunnel is disconnected, hold each local accept for up to this many milliseconds instead of refusing it at once (ADR\-0023 decision 19), sending nothing for it until the tunnel\*(Aqs connection is back. `0` (the default) refuses at once. Needs a nonzero `\-\-supervise`; bound `0..=2000`; not valid with `\-\-remote`. Full semantics: `docs/CLI.md` §6.9
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR
Never install or upgrade `qsh` on the remote host, and do not ask; a missing `qsh` ends as `remote_qsh_missing`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR
Never start a hole punch on this command\*(Aqs behalf, and send no `PunchRequest` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 19). The route is the direct dial alone. Wins over `[punch].mode`
.TP
\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR \fI<PEER>\fR
Use this pinned peer as the rendezvous for hole punching on this command, instead of the host\*(Aqs own `rendezvous` hints (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 4). Must be pinned with an address. Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-jump\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Reach the host through these pinned peers, first hop first, for this command: `a` or `a,b` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0056 decision 20). Each hop is the name of a pin; a `user@` or `:port` on it is ignored by the QUIC path. `none` goes the ordinary way even when the host\*(Aqs pin has a `jump`. Wins over `\-o ProxyJump` and over the pin\*(Aqs own `jump`. Not stored. The short `\-J` is the same flag on the commands that take it
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIHOST\fR>
Host alias from the trust store (`qsh trust list`)
.SH VERSION
v0.5.8
